WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.977
Rust applications often wrap resources
that are not just ordinary memory:

2
00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:07.037
file descriptors, sockets, database
handles, device handles,

3
00:00:07.117 --> 00:00:09.711
temporary directories, native handles,
lock objects,

4
00:00:09.791 --> 00:00:11.391
or externally allocated buffers.

5
00:00:11.471 --> 00:00:14.783
A safe abstraction should make ownership
and cleanup authority clear.

6
00:00:14.863 --> 00:00:16.850
Private fields are a powerful design tool.

7
00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.020
If callers cannot manufacture duplicate
owners from raw handles,

8
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.694
they are less likely to create duplicate
cleanup paths.

9
00:00:22.774 --> 00:00:26.141
Constructors can validate that a handle is
valid, establish ownership,

10
00:00:26.221 --> 00:00:29.587
and return a wrapper that controls how the
resource is used and released.

11
00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:33.365
Methods should expose controlled
operations rather than exposing raw cleanup

12
00:00:33.445 --> 00:00:34.052
mechanisms.

13
00:00:34.132 --> 00:00:37.167
A resource wrapper can provide read,
write, query, close, flush,

14
00:00:37.247 --> 00:00:41.000
or shutdown behavior while keeping the
actual release contract inside the type.

15
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.668
`Drop` can then release the resource once
when the wrapper's ownership ends.

16
00:00:44.748 --> 00:00:47.617
Some external resources provide idempotent
close behavior,

17
00:00:47.697 --> 00:00:49.684
where repeated close calls are tolerated.

18
00:00:49.764 --> 00:00:50.647
That can be useful,

19
00:00:50.727 --> 00:00:53.818
but idempotence should not be a substitute
for ownership clarity.

20
00:00:53.898 --> 00:00:56.602
If code depends on repeated cleanup calls
being harmless,

21
00:00:56.682 --> 00:01:00.490
the lifecycle design is harder to review
and may fail when the underlying resource

22
00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:01.012
changes.

23
00:01:01.092 --> 00:01:04.844
A good resource abstraction states who
owns the resource, when cleanup happens,

24
00:01:04.924 --> 00:01:07.463
what happens on error, whether early close
is allowed,

25
00:01:07.543 --> 00:01:10.082
and what methods remain valid after close
or shutdown.

26
00:01:10.162 --> 00:01:13.915
The safe API should make duplicate cleanup
difficult even when callers are under

27
00:01:13.995 --> 00:01:15.540
pressure during error handling.
